In this lesson, we'll look at how ratios and rates pop up whenever money is involved, from supermarket shelves to your weekly pay. First up is best buy. When two products come in different sized packs, you can't just compare the sticker price. Instead, find the unit rate, that is the cost per single unit, like per kilogram, per litre, or per one hundred grams, and pick whichever one is cheaper. Next, wages. Your pay is just a rate too. You take the rate per hour and multiply it by the number of hours you work, and that's what you earn. Finally, fuel costs. The cost of a trip is simply the fuel price per litre multiplied by the litres you use. So if a car burns ten litres on a journey and fuel costs a dollar forty a litre, that trip costs fourteen dollars. The trick in all three is spotting the rate, then multiplying or dividing to get what you need. Now let's see it in action.